REACT COMPONENT ARCHITECTURE
============================

1. COMPONENT LAYERS
-------------------

The frontend is organized into four layers:

1. Layout Components
2. UI Components (Reusable)
3. Feature Components (Domain-specific)
4. Page Components (Route-level)

Each layer has strict responsibilities.


2. LAYOUT COMPONENTS
--------------------

Purpose:
- Provide global structure
- Wrap pages with navigation, headers, footers, sidebars

Examples:
- <Header />
- <Footer />
- <Sidebar />
- <MemberSidebar />
- <AdminSidebar />

Rules:
- No business logic
- No API calls
- Only layout, navigation, and role-aware menus


3. UI COMPONENTS (REUSABLE)
---------------------------

Purpose:
- Generic building blocks used across all features

Examples:
- <Button />
- <Card />
- <Modal />
- <Table />
- <FormField />
- <LoadingSpinner />
- <ErrorMessage />
- <Breadcrumbs />

Rules:
- No domain-specific logic
- No API calls
- Must be pure and reusable
- Accept props only, no global state


4. FEATURE COMPONENTS (DOMAIN-SPECIFIC)
---------------------------------------

Purpose:
- Encapsulate logic for each domain (news, events, programs, admin tools)

Examples:
- NewsList
- EventDetail
- MemberProfileForm
- AdminEventEditor
- ProgramVolunteerForm

Rules:
- May call APIs
- May use feature-specific state
- Must NOT contain routing
- Must NOT contain layout
- Must NOT contain global state


5. PAGE COMPONENTS (ROUTE-LEVEL)
--------------------------------

Purpose:
- Map directly to routes in React Router
- Compose layout + feature components
- Handle role-based access

Examples:
- Home.jsx
- MemberDashboard.jsx
- AdminDashboard.jsx
- EventsList.jsx
- AdminEvents.jsx

Rules:
- No business logic
- No API calls (except initial fetch via hooks)
- Should be thin wrappers around feature components


6. STATE MANAGEMENT STRATEGY
----------------------------

Global State (Context):
- Auth state
- User roles
- Theme
- API client config

Feature State (Hooks):
- useEvents()
- usePrograms()
- useMembers()
- useMarket()
- useVoting()

Local State:
- Form inputs
- UI toggles
- Pagination controls


7. API INTEGRATION PATTERN
--------------------------

Each feature has its own API module:

/api
  publicApi.js
  memberApi.js
  adminApi.js
  eventsApi.js
  programsApi.js
  mediaApi.js
  marketApi.js
  votingApi.js

Rules:
- Page components call hooks
- Hooks call API modules
- API modules call httpClient.js
- httpClient handles auth tokens & errors


8. ROLE-AWARE ROUTING
---------------------

Use <AuthGuard /> and <RoleGuard /> components:

<AuthGuard>
  <RoleGuard roles={['admin', 'grand_knight']}>
    <AdminDashboard />
  </RoleGuard>
</AuthGuard>

Rules:
- Page components must not check roles directly
- Guards wrap routes only
- Guards use AuthContext


9. FEATURE MODULE STRUCTURE
---------------------------

Each feature folder contains:

feature/
  pages/
  components/
  hooks/
  api/
  utils/

Example:

features/events/
  pages/
    EventsList.jsx
    EventDetail.jsx
  components/
    EventCard.jsx
    VolunteerForm.jsx
  hooks/
    useEvents.js
  api/
    eventsApi.js
  utils/
    eventHelpers.js


10. NAMING CONVENTIONS
----------------------

Components:
- PascalCase (EventDetail.jsx)

Hooks:
- camelCase starting with "use" (useEvents.js)

API modules:
- camelCase ending with Api (eventsApi.js)

Pages:
- PascalCase matching route (MemberDashboard.jsx)

Feature folders:
- lowercase (events, programs, admin)


11. REUSABILITY RULES
---------------------

UI components:
- Must be reusable across all features

Feature components:
- Reusable only within the same domain

Page components:
- Never reused; route-specific


12. ERROR & LOADING HANDLING
----------------------------

Every feature hook returns:

{
  data,
  isLoading,
  error,
  refetch
}

Pages must handle:

<LoadingSpinner />
<ErrorMessage />

No feature component should render loading or error states.


13. FORM ARCHITECTURE
---------------------

Use a shared <FormField /> component.

Forms follow:

- Controlled inputs
- Validation via utils/validators.js
- Submission via feature hooks
- Errors displayed via <ErrorMessage />


14. DIRECTORY STRUCTURE SUMMARY
-------------------------------

/src
  app/
  assets/
  components/
    layout/
    ui/
    common/
  features/
    public/
    member/
    admin/
  api/
  utils/

This structure scales indefinitely.


15. COMPONENT RESPONSIBILITY SUMMARY
------------------------------------

Layer         | Can Fetch Data | Can Hold Global State | Can Hold Feature State | Can Render Layout
--------------|----------------|------------------------|------------------------|-------------------
Layout        | No             | No                     | No                     | Yes
UI Components | No             | No                     | No                     | No
Feature       | Yes            | No                     | Yes                    | No
Pages         | No             | No                     | Minimal                | Yes


16. EXAMPLE COMPONENT FLOW
--------------------------

Route → Page → Feature Component → Hook → API → Backend → Postgres

Example:

/member/events → MemberEvents.jsx → EventsList → useEvents → eventsApi → FastAPI → Postgres


17. ROLE-BASED UI VISIBILITY
----------------------------

Use <RoleGuard> inside layout components to hide/show menu items.

Example:

{roles.includes('admin') && <AdminSidebar />}


18. TESTING STRATEGY
--------------------

Unit tests:
- UI components
- Feature components

Integration tests:
- Hooks
- API modules

E2E tests:
- Page components
- Routing
- Role guards


19. PERFORMANCE RULES
---------------------

- Use React.memo for UI components
- Use Suspense for lazy-loaded pages
- Use pagination for large lists
- Use debounced search inputs
- Use code splitting for admin features


20. ACCESSIBILITY RULES
-----------------------

- All UI components must support ARIA attributes
- All forms must have labels
- All images must have alt text
- All modals must trap focus